# Practice Exercise Rulebook — Everyday Drills + Ticket-Work Job Simulations

Use this rule when the user asks you to **produce or extend practice material** for a programming course (`c-beg-to-adv`, `cpp-beg-to-adv`, or a future course tree).

The user **runs the curriculum manually** via `course-subjects.txt`. Your job is to **materialize** that list into two complementary deliverable families that share the same EE/product narrative but serve different learning modes.

---

## 0. Canonical inputs (read first, every time)

| File | Role |
|------|------|
| `course-subjects.txt` | **Authoritative ordered subject list.** One subject per line. Do not skip, reorder, or invent topics not on the list. |
| `exercises-instructions.txt` | Batch/workbook protocol (5–7 exercises per batch, cumulative mix, EE framing). |
| This rulebook | Architecture for **everyday** + **ticket-work** deliverables, HTML/test conventions, level scale. |

**Pre-flight checklist (mandatory):**

1. Read `course-subjects.txt` end-to-end. Count active subjects (exclude blank lines and “MISSING FOR NOW” blocks unless the user updated the file).
2. Note **forbidden / deferred** topics in that file (e.g. STL sections marked missing in an early C++ pass).
3. Confirm **language** (C vs C++) and **standard** (C11/C17 vs C++20) with the repo you are in.
4. Confirm whether you are generating **everyday**, **ticket-work**, or **both**.

---

## 1. Two deliverable families (always paired conceptually)

For every course repo, the user expects **both**:

### A. Everyday practice — subject-by-subject drills

**Purpose:** One focused exercise **per line** in `course-subjects.txt`. Straightforward, isolated practice. No intentional multi-bug codebase.

**Path pattern:**

```
<course-root>/everyday-practice/          # volume 1
<course-root>/everyday-practice-2/        # optional second pass (new tasks, same order)
```

**Deliverable:** Standalone HTML (`per_subject_practice.html` or numbered volumes).

**Hard rules:**

- **Every active subject gets exactly one exercise.** Zero skips.
- Subject order in HTML **matches** `course-subjects.txt` order.
- Number exercises (`01`, `02`, …) and anchor IDs (`#s01`, `#s02`, …).
- Include a **table of contents** linking all subjects.
- Each exercise: **task statement only** in the visible body; **full solution + brief “why this feature”** inside a **Reveal solution** button panel only.
- Simpler than ticket-work; may be pencil-and-paper or small snippet — does not require the core-system repo unless the user asks.

### B. Ticket-work — 3-level job simulation

**Purpose:** A **coherent embedded product codebase** with **intentional bugs**, QA-style HTML tickets, and a **per-ticket automated test runner**. Mimics real on-the-job defect triage.

**Path pattern:**

```
<course-root>/ticket-work/
  entry-level/ticket_notebook.html      # Level 1 (see §2)
  mid-level/ticket_notebook.html        # Level 2
  staff-level/ticket_notebook.html      # Level 3 (or senior-level/ if legacy name)
  <core-system>/                      # e.g. vlc-core-system, dsp-core-system
    include/...
    src/...
    tests/ticket_tests.cpp
    tests/ticket_test_common.hpp
    CMakeLists.txt
    README.md
    TICKET_CROSSWALK.md
```

**Hard rules:**

- **5 tickets per level** (15 total), difficulty increases **within** each notebook and **across** levels.
- Each ticket maps to **one primary defect** in named source file(s).
- Codebase ships with **bugs present** (not pre-solved).
- Each ticket has a **dedicated test** invokable in isolation.
- Do **not** put solutions or fix instructions in test stdout — only **observed**, **expected**, **PASS** / **FAIL** (see §6).

