## 5. Ticket HTML notebook design

Each level: **one** standalone `ticket_notebook.html`.

### 5.1 Page sections

1. **Header** — level badge, ticket count, language/standard note.
2. **System intro** — product blurb, student role, path to `<core-system>/`, build + test commands.
3. **One `<article class="ticket">` per ticket**, containing the blocks below.
4. **Footer** — product name + ticket count.

### 5.2 Per-ticket blocks (required order)

| Block | CSS class | Content rules |
|-------|-----------|---------------|
| **Problem** | `.section` | QA symptom in plain English. Steps / expected vs actual behaviour. **No fix spoilers.** |
| **Fix (bug is here)** | `.section.fix` | **Files + symbol names only.** Example: `` `src/lab_curve.cpp` — `LabCurve::eval(double)` ``. **FORBIDDEN in Fix text:** “must be virtual”, “use norm=false”, “add delete[]”, “return *this”, code snippets, or any wording that states the solution. |
| **Reproduce** | `.section.test` | Exact test command + **bug output** (observed/expected lines students will see before fixing). |
| **Pass criteria** | `.pass` | e.g. `XXX-ENTRY-101 PASS` and exit code 0. |
| **Hint button** | `.panel.hint-panel` | Nudge only; no full solution. |
| **Solution button** | `.panel.sol-panel` | Root cause + full fixed code + short review note. |

### 5.3 Fix-section examples

**Good:**

> `include/vlc/icodec.hpp` — `ICodec` destructor and `NotchCodec` teardown path.

**Bad (do not write):**

> `ICodec::~ICodec` must be `virtual`.

> Change `eval(x, true)` to `eval(x, false)`.

### 5.4 Styling

- Entry / Mid / Staff use **distinct accent colours** (green → amber → red, or course palette).
- Reuse the ticket-work CSS structure from `cpp-beg-to-adv/ticket-work/` for consistency.
- Keep max-width ~960px, monospace for IDs and test output.

### 5.5 Ticket IDs

Format: `<PREFIX>-<LEVEL>-<nnn>`

Examples: `VLC-ENTRY-101`, `DSP-MID-204`, `AFE-STAFF-305`.

Use stable prefixes per product (`VLC`, `DSP`, etc.).

---

## 6. Everyday HTML notebook design

### 6.1 Structure

- One `<section class="subject">` per `course-subjects.txt` line.
- Fields: subject number, **exact subject title from file**, task paragraph, **Reveal solution** button, hidden solution panel.

### 6.2 Task writing

- One clear job per subject — typical use case for that language feature.
- Prefer EE context: buffers, samples, registers, gain, PSD, comb delay.
- Match difficulty to **that subject only**; cross-subject mixing is optional light review, not required.

### 6.3 Solutions

- Full code in the hidden panel.
- Short “why” tying solution to the curriculum line.
- **Never** place solution code in the visible task area.

### 6.4 STL / phased curriculum

When `course-subjects.txt` marks STL as separate trailing subjects:

- Everyday volume 1: non-STL subjects only, honour “MISSING FOR NOW”.
- Optional separate everyday volume or workbook for STL blocks (vectors, list, algorithm, maps) — **one exercise per STL line**, still no skips.

